this is a production  of Cornell University  it's March 11th 2014 and we have had a  very cold winter I'm at the Lansing  research farm we hit minus 10 here on  January 22nd and then had several other  instances where we went below zero so  Adam and I are out here cutting buds to  see what's alive and what's dead and so  I wanted to show you how this process  works and so what we do is we use a  razor blade and cut a way up buds and  look at what's alive inside and so we're  gonna zoom up on a bud here and you need  to remember that great buds are what we  call compound buds and so within each  bud that we cut there's actually primary  a secondary and a tertiary bud and I  found a great example here for what's  alive so what I've done is come in here  and that's what the bud looked like I  sliced it with the razor blade opened it  up and you can actually see the three  buds here this Center one is the primary  and there is the secondary and then  there's a little green dot over here and  that's the tertiary bud so this is great  we can look at the other side of the bud  here too and you can see those green  dots those are buds that are still alive  after this winter they all have the  potential to produce shoots so what will  happen is the primary will grow first  and as long as it grows well it's  unlikely the secondary or the tertiary  will ever grow if we move up the clone  here is another bud that I cut and in  this you can see that the center of that  is actually brown and so that's our  primary bud the primary bud is dead it's  been damaged by the cold but you can see  a little bit of green on either side of  that that's the secondary and the  tertiary and those are both alive  so this is what we do pre pruning if  we've had some cold damage is that we go  through we actually cut these buds with  a razor blade and look at what  proportion are alive and what proportion  are dead because that will tell us how  many buds we want to leave on the vine  because I'll know for example that to  balance this vine I want to have in this  example  thirdly live shoots but if I cut news  and I know that only about half of them  are alive that means I actually moved to  live 6 2 nodes rather than 30 to get my  30 shoots through the growing season so  in many years if we haven't had cold  damage we don't bother doing this but in  a year like this where we're seeing a  lot of damage in the Finger Lakes we go  through and cut some buds first so we  get a sample and figure out what's alive  and what's dead so we know how many more  buds we need to leave when we do our  pruning  so it's March 11th 2014 and I'm in the  Lansing vineyard we've had a very cold  winter and we've had some blood damage  that I wanted to show you so I've cut  some buds here and this is something  that we frequently do before pruning if  we've had cold events because we want to  see how many of our buds are alive and  how many of our buds are dead because  the number of buds were going to leave  on a vine well know the number we moved  to have a balanced vine for example it  might be 30 in this situation and so if  I know half the buds are dead I've got  to leave sixty buds so that I have third  issues so I've cut some buds here and I  wanted to show you you need to remember  that great buds are actually compound  buds and so within everybody there's  actually three smaller buds and this is  one where everything's dead but you can  see a bigger set of circles here that's  the primary bud this is the secondary  bud and this little one over here this  little wedge is called the tertiary bud  and on this one when we cut it open all  three are dead so the primary the  secondary and the tertiary are all brown  nothing is growing from that bud this  year the second one here it's a little  harder to see because the primary  secondary and tertiary are actually  alive here so this little middle green  point here this is our primary bud it's  alive and well if I had not cut it it  would grow into a chute there is our  secondary bud and there is our tertiary  over here which is alive too  so normally it's the primary that will  die first so it's easier to find buds  here where the primary is dead but the  secondary is alive and in that case this  secondary bud would actually grow and be  a chute in many cases it's not as  fruitful as a primary chute the fruit  will come out a couple of weeks later  and so it has less time to mature  through the growing season sometimes it  won't be a fruitful chute but at least  there'll be a chute there if the  secondary is alive and the primary is  not but in this case we're hoping to  find lots of buds like this because we  want to see an alive primary secondary  and tertiary bud this has been a  production of Cornell University on the  web at  Cornell edu
 

